  "query_preview": "..\n\nA jet exit region and upper level confluent flow will migrate \ntowards the Canadian Maritimes during the next 36 hours. The 1000-\n500mb ridge will amplify over Lower Michigan, dampening the effects \nof a deep shortwave trough lifting out of the Northern Plains to \nJames Bay.  Precipitation free conditions can be expected for the \nremainder of today ahead of the cold front along with temperatures \nsome 15 degrees above normal. A relatively dry cold front draped \nfrom the shortwave will push across the forecast area between 18z \nThu - 01z Fri.\n\nRelative humidity progs in the plan view show an extremely narrow \nmoisture starved boundary as it pushes across the area, limited to \nonly a few tens of miles across. The current forecast has likely \npops of 50-60% percent between 18-00z which is adequate. The overall \nageostrophic circulation is expected to be very weak with lean 850-\n700mb frontogenesis. Forecast soundings show static stability \nlingering between 4.0 -7.0 kft agl limiting coverage of activity \nwith MUCAPES of a few hundred J/kg. Latest Swody2 has now \nintroduced General Thunderstorm designation for Southeast Michigan.\nWarm temperatures are anticipated Thursday ahead of the front with \nreadings pushing into the lower 70s.\n\nAnticyclonic curvature to the zonal jet streak in portions of \ncentral Canada will lead to strongly anticyclonic flow curvature \nover the Great Lakes Friday and Saturday. High confidence exists in \nstrong static stability between 3.0 and 17.0 kft agl. Southeast \nMichigan will reside in the gradient to the 1000-500mb thickness \ntempering high temperatures both Friday and Saturday in the lower \n60s.\n\nLongwave ridge is forecasted to build across the region this weekend \nand then continue into the beginning of next week. Current high \ntemperature forecasts suggest temperatures reaching the middle 60s \nsome 7 to 10 degrees above normal. Any pattern change with the \narrival of Pacific energy appears to hold off until after Day 7, \nTuesday.",
